<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eric of Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1382–<a href="/years/1459">1459</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">House of Griffins</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, a Pomeranian duke's son and great-nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a>, was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar">Kalmar</a> in 1397, the first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. He introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> Toll and fought the counts of Holstein. Deposed in the 1430s, he spent his last years as duke at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Rügenwalde</a> in Pomerania-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania.jpg" alt="Eric of Pomerania" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erik_I,_1382-1459,_hertig_av_Pommern_konung_av_Danmark_Norge_och_Sverige_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15058.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Erik was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Darłowo</a>, then called Rügenwalde, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. He was named Bogislaw at birth and was the son of Wartislaw VII, Duke of Pomerania. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a>, his great-aunt, wanted her three kingdoms to remain united and chose him as her heir. In 1389 he was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to be raised by her, and his name was changed to the more Nordic-sounding Erik. On 8 September 1389 he was hailed as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> at the Ting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>.</p><p>He was proclaimed king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1396, and on 17 June 1397 he was crowned king of the three countries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar">Kalmar</a> cathedral. A union treaty drafted at the same time established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>, although Margaret remained the de facto ruler until her death in 1412.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1417 Erik made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> a royal possession, which secured its status as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. From 1423 to May 1425 he made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, where he was dubbed a Knight of the Holy Sepulchre. Almost the whole of his sole rule was shaped by conflict with the counts of Schauenburg and Holstein over South Jutland. His choice of war over negotiation led to a devastating conflict that ended without conquests and cost him the areas he had won.</p><p>In 1429 he introduced the Sound Dues, paid by all ships passing through the Sound into or out of the Baltic, which lasted until 1857. He built the fortress of Krogen at the narrowest point to enforce them. They gave the crown a large, stable income and helped the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Elsinore</a> flourish, but they challenged the Baltic powers, especially the Hanseatic cities. From 1426 to 1435 he was at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> and Holstein, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vordingborg">Peace of Vordingborg</a> of 17 July 1435 exempted the Hanseatic cities from the dues and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> to the count of Holstein. In 1434 Swedish farmers and mine workers rose in the Engelbrekt rebellion, which the Swedish nobility used to weaken him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the Danish nobility refused to accept his choice of Bogislaw IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> as his successor, Erik left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> for Visborg Castle on Gotland. The councils of Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> deposed him in 1439, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> followed in 1440, with a formal deposition in 1442. His nephew Christopher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was chosen as king.</p><p>From 1449 to 1459 Erik ruled Pomerania-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Rügenwalde</a>, a small partition of Pomerania-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a>, as Erik I. He died at Darłowo Castle in 1459 and was buried in St Mary's Church there. His epithet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was a pejorative, meant to suggest that he did not belong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erik of Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Griffin prince, <b>Eric of Pomerania</b>, became king of Denmark, Norway and Sweden in 1397, and later ended his days as a minor duke in Pomerania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Eric of Pomerania</b> introduced the Sound Toll around 1429, a levy on ships passing Helsingør that helped fund the crown until its abolition in 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Engelbrekt, who had led the rising of 1434 against <b>Eric of Pomerania</b>, was killed on an islet in Lake Hjälmaren by the nobleman Måns Bengtsson, whose father had a legal dispute with him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Eric of Pomerania</b>, grandson of Bogislaw V, was elected king of the Kalmar Union in 1397.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stolp">Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1397 her great-nephew <b>Eric of Pomerania</b> was crowned king of all three realms at Kalmar.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Darłowo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Darłowo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.42,16.41&amp;year=1432" aria-label="Darłowo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Eric of Pomerania"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4330 5407h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4330 5407h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.42,16.41&amp;year=1432">What was here in 1432? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
